WEBVTT

00:00.900 --> 00:06.342
Teach the Machine. How a machine learns from
examples, and why the data matters.

00:08.542 --> 00:13.111
In this simulator, the question is simple: is
the mango ripe?

00:13.161 --> 00:18.867
But nobody is going to write the rule. The
machine will learn from examples.

00:19.717 --> 00:22.499
Each mango becomes a point on this map.

00:22.549 --> 00:26.275
Across, the color of the skin, from green to
yellow.

00:26.325 --> 00:30.413
Up and down, the firmness, from soft fruit to
hard fruit.

00:31.263 --> 00:36.534
Let me teach it. I mark a few unripe mangoes,
with green skin and hard.

00:36.584 --> 00:39.306
Now a few ripe ones, yellow and soft.

00:39.356 --> 00:42.754
Look: the background of the map colors itself.

00:42.804 --> 00:46.201
That coloring is the rule the machine learned.

00:47.051 --> 00:53.527
With forty varied examples, I test the machine
on two hundred mangoes it has never seen.

00:53.577 --> 00:55.678
Ninety-three percent correct.

00:56.528 --> 01:00.342
But how does it decide? I ask about a new
mango.

01:00.392 --> 01:08.072
The machine calculates the distance to each example,
picks the five closest ones, and they vote.

01:08.122 --> 01:11.051
Two unripe, three ripe. Answer: ripe.

01:11.901 --> 01:13.629
Why measure the distance?

01:13.679 --> 01:17.752
To know who the neighbors are, the machine
needs a number.

01:17.802 --> 01:28.131
It uses the Pythagorean theorem: the difference in skin color, the difference in
firmness, and the straight-line distance between the two mangoes.

01:28.181 --> 01:30.761
For the machine, similar means close.

01:31.611 --> 01:33.953
And what if one example is wrong?

01:34.003 --> 01:37.721
I put a ripe mango in the middle of the unripe
ones.

01:37.771 --> 01:45.909
Listening only to the nearest neighbor, the machine creates an
island around the mistake: it memorized the error.

01:45.959 --> 01:51.344
With three neighbors, the mistake loses the
vote and the island disappears.

01:52.194 --> 01:55.334
And what if it listens to too many neighbors?

01:55.384 --> 02:02.777
With fifteen, the boundary gets blurred and the accuracy
drops from ninety-three to eighty-seven percent.

02:02.827 --> 02:09.581
Not memorizing every example, and not erasing the details
either: choosing k is part of the job.

02:10.431 --> 02:14.678
Now the same machine, trained with an
unbalanced sample.

02:14.728 --> 02:18.591
Thirty-four unripe mangoes, and only six ripe
ones.

02:18.641 --> 02:23.272
All with green or half-yellow skin. Every
example is correct.

02:23.322 --> 02:26.571
And the result drops to fifty-five percent.

02:26.621 --> 02:30.178
The errors cluster where examples were
missing.

02:30.228 --> 02:33.400
Unbalanced data produces a biased machine.

02:34.250 --> 02:39.172
Finally, replace the mango with a face, a
résumé, or a loan application.

02:39.222 --> 02:45.800
The people left out of the data are the ones the machine
gets wrong more often, without warning.

02:45.850 --> 02:54.432
In the classroom, the question that matters is not what the artificial
intelligence answered, but where the answer came from.
